## The idea

Keep every scheduled task in a **priority queue ordered by its due time**, so the next task to run is always at the top. One background **worker thread** loops: look at the top task; if it is due, take it out and run it; if not, sleep until it is due. When someone schedules a new task, we **wake the worker** — the new task may be due earlier than the one it was sleeping for. The task itself runs **outside the lock**, so a slow task never blocks `Schedule`.

## Step by step

1. Store each task as a Handle with its action and the clock time it is due. Put it in a PriorityQueue keyed by (due time, sequence number) — the sequence number keeps tasks with the same due time in the order they were scheduled.
2. Schedule: under the lock, refuse if shut down, add the task, then `Monitor.PulseAll` to wake the worker.
3. Worker: under the lock, peek the top task. Cancelled → throw it away. Due → take it out and leave the lock. Not due → `Monitor.Wait(lock, millisecondsLeft)`, which releases the lock while sleeping and wakes early on a pulse.
4. Run the task outside the lock inside try/catch, so one throwing task cannot kill the worker.
5. Cancel: under the lock, mark the handle cancelled if it has not started yet. The worker skips it when it reaches the top.
6. Shutdown: set a flag and wake the worker. With drainPending the worker keeps running until the queue is empty; without it, it stops at once. A ManualResetEventSlim is set when the worker exits, and AwaitTermination waits on it.

## Worked example

**Input:** At time 0: Schedule(A, 150 ms), Schedule(B, 40 ms), Schedule(C, 90 ms)

| Step | What happens |
|---|---|
| after the three Schedules | queue by due time: B@40, C@90, A@150; worker wakes on each pulse |
| worker at t=0 | top is B, due in 40 ms → Wait(40) |
| t=40 | B is due → dequeue, run B outside the lock |
| t=40 | top is C, due in 50 ms → Wait(50) |
| t=90 | run C; top is A → Wait(60) |
| t=150 | run A; queue empty → Wait() with no timeout until the next pulse |

**Result:** B, C, A — deadline order, not scheduling order

## Cost

Schedule is O(log n) (heap insert). Each run is O(log n) (heap remove). Cancel is O(1) because a cancelled task stays in the heap until it reaches the top — memory is O(scheduled tasks, including cancelled ones not yet reached).

